  "text" : "Cosine similarity is just the dot product with both lengths divided out.\nProjection divides out only one. Here's when you want each.\nNow all three start from the same thing. The dot product, alignment with the length still in it.\nCosine similarity divides by both lengths. Both magnitudes go away and you're left with pure direction.\nA number between -1 and 1. And that's what you use to compare embeddings.\nNow projection divides by only the target's length. Picture casting a shadow from one vector onto another.\nAnd projection asks, \"How much of you points along this direction?\"\nThe shadow keeps the original's length in the answer and it splits the vector in two,\nthe part along the direction and the part left over.\nEven coordinates are projections, one onto each basis direction.\nThe dot product is alignment with the length, cosine is alignment without them.\nProjection is how much of you lies along that line."
